{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission-review-c10c.json\", \"summary\": \"Turn 67 reviewed the third codec scenario, the one that turns the truncation into a receiver crash.\\n\\nState on entry. The first two C10 artifacts are confirmed (the value truncation and the shifted following command). The third, 3ee262e5afd44c419b561c23376ffafb (executed as 47fd9044…), reported violated with the oracle correspondence unreviewed; its stream shows the real PreAccept marshalled from two commands with an oversized first value, then message_shifted with panicked true, panic value \\\"makeslice: len out of range\\\" and 15700 bytes left.\\n\\nReview judgement (checker_correspondence, no_issue_found). Requirement versus predicate: the claim is that a value the client can build must survive the codec or be rejected, and this scenario asserts that decoding a message the implementation itself marshalled does not panic, evaluated on the decoder's own behaviour with the panic value and leftover bytes printed. Entry and legality: the message is a PreAccept — the form a leader sends for a proposal, carrying commands in the codec's array form — so the input is the protocol's own message rather than a synthetic buffer; only the decode is wrapped in recover, which is what makes the panic observable, and the handler bytes are untouched. Oracle and identity: panicked, the panic value and the remaining bytes are read after the decode, and the message identity correlates prerequisite and result. Inference to a receiver crash: the replica reader unmarshals from its peer stream without a recover, so a panic in the unmarshaller propagates and ends the process; that step is recorded as a source-based inference rather than an observation. Limits are explicit: the shipped configuration's 1000-byte commands never reach the boundary, no replica reader is driven so the stream's state after a panic is unobserved, and one message type with one oversized command is exercised. No counterevidence was found that the decoder survives in this scope.\\n\\nEffect on the run's picture. The C10 finding now has three scenarios of increasing severity from one root cause — a truncated value, a corrupted following command, and a panic in the receiver's decoder — with importance bounded by the configured command size but the mechanism fully recorded. Audit spec version 13; the nine other settled claims (C1 re-confirmed on the amended claim, C2, C3, C4, C5, C6, C7, C8, C9) and the two paused candidates are untouched, and the only visible administrative item remains the structurally stuck scope issue on the superseded C1 artifact. No requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened.\"}"}}
